WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.440
I have often felt
like a fish out of water,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.280
at various points
throughout my career.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.960
But I've definitely come
to the realisation that my diversity,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.920
and the difference that I bring,
is a real strength.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.080
My first tip is...

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.360
It can weigh heavily
on your shoulders

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.600
and it can be quite frightening,
I think, to take a risk

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.440
and to do something different
and to break new ground.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.080
Sometimes people will question you

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.200
and question whether
you're making the right decisions.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.760
And sometimes you'll
question yourself, but that's okay.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.240
My family come from
East Africa and India,

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.600
and so they are the children
of Britain's colonial past.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.000
When I said to some members
of my extended family

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.200
that I was going to
work for the Foreign Office

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.200
there was an immediate sense of,

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.840
"How could you do this?

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.160
"Do you not remember
what they did to our people?"

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.880
But I think you need to recognise
that if you break new ground

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.440
there will be people
who question you,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.320
but if you believe it's the right
thing to do, you need to persevere.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.880
So my next tip is...

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.200
So I know that when I'm in new,
uncomfortable situations

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.360
I get a strong sense of fear,
and my flight mechanism kicks in.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.280
And for me, that starts
with butterflies in my tummy,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.160
and it goes on to
trying to think through,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.520
"How can I get out
of this situation?"

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.200
So when I joined GCHQ,
I definitely felt in those early days

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.880
that sense of fear and flight.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.040
And I'm surrounded by
some incredibly intelligent people

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.280
and they're gonna
find me out pretty quickly.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.640
I've learnt, over the years,
that actually that feeling

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.600
only stays with me for
a relatively short period of time,

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.440
and if I ride it out and if I accept
fear, that actually it will be fine,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.440
and the rational side
will kick in soon enough.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.120
...that's my next top tip.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.880
Because it's only through asking

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.040
and really listening
to what people say

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.680
that you can build relationships
of trust and confidence.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.800
And in my job, I often meet people
from international backgrounds,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.640
and the one thing that I always do

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.760
is to look at
what's on the front page

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.600
of their local newspaper that day.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.320
Because that gives me something
topical to talk to them about.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.360
What you're trying to do
is to make people feel

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.680
that you're really interested in them

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.480
and what they have to say to you.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.840
My final tip is...

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.920
I think it's really, really important
when you're breaking new ground

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.680
to find somebody who will
encourage you to be yourself,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.920
and will advocate for you.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.000
For me, I was lucky that I was able
to find a champion

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.880
fairly early on in my career,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.680
and she's somebody that I will turn
to and ask for advice and guidance.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.120
But also, don't forget
that you are a champion yourself.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.400
I learnt something which was
"lift as you climb,"

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.440
so making sure that you're helping
somebody else to succeed.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.880
Part of what drives me
is proving people wrong.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.320
The people who said that I couldn't
possibly work for the Foreign Office,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.960
because it wasn't for people
from my background.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.360
So success for me

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.720
isn't about the glory of being
at the top of an organisation,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.920
it's about having
the maximum impact that I can,

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.440
but also about being an inspiration.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.560
So if I can change the conversations,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.200
and encourage people to dare
to be different and to take a risk,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.320
then I feel like
I will have succeeded.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:42.560
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

